window.SF_DOCS_INDEX = [{"t":"SessionForge documentation","g":"Getting started","u":"docs/","h":[],"x":"SessionForge is a free, open-source desktop browser that keeps each logged-in user in its own isolated session, shows them side by side, and lets your coding agent use them through a local MCP server. Getting started Install SessionForge on macOS, Windows or Linux and set up your first sessions. Using SessionForge Sessions, split view, environment safety, shortcuts and Google sign-in. Chrome extension Send a Chrome login to SessionForge in one click, and sign in with Chrome. MCP Server / Agent Bridge Connect Cursor, Codex or Claude Code, the 17 tools, and the security model. Project Roadmap, contributing, privacy and security. These pages summarise the README and the other files in the repository. Where a page says Planned , the feature is not in a release yet."},{"t":"Install SessionForge","g":"Getting started","u":"docs/install/","h":[["Prebuilt downloads","prebuilt-downloads"],["macOS first launch","macos-first-launch"],["Windows first launch","windows-first-launch"],["Linux","linux"],["Run from source","run-from-source"]],"x":"Get the file for your system from GitHub Releases, or run SessionForge from source. Prebuilt downloads # Download from GitHub Releases . SHA256SUMS.txt in the release lists the checksums. System File macOS, Apple Silicon SessionForge-<version>-mac-arm64.dmg macOS, Intel SessionForge-<version>-mac-x64.dmg Windows 10/11 SessionForge-<version>-win-x64-setup.exe Linux (Debian, Ubuntu) SessionForge-<version>-linux-amd64.deb Linux (other distributions) SessionForge-<version>-linux-x86_64.AppImage macOS first launch # The app is ad-hoc signed but not notarized (there is no Apple Developer account yet), so macOS blocks the first open. Drag SessionForge to Applications and open it once. Then either click Open Anyway in System Settings → Privacy & Security (required on macOS 15 and later), or run: sh Copy xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/SessionForge.app Windows first launch # The installer is unsigned, so SmartScreen shows \"Windows protected your PC\". Click More info → Run anyway . Linux # On Ubuntu 23.10 and later, use the .deb. Its install script sets up Chromium's sandbox helper, so the app starts with the sandbox on. sh Copy sudo apt install ./SessionForge-*-linux-amd64.deb The AppImage works on distributions that allow unprivileged user namespaces: sh Copy chmod +x SessionForge-*.AppImage && ./SessionForge-*.AppImage Avoid --no-sandbox . On Ubuntu 23.10 and later AppArmor blocks those namespaces and the AppImage exits with a SUID sandbox error. --no-sandbox starts it, but turns off Chromium's sandbox for every page you open. Prefer the .deb. Run from source # Needs Node.js 20 or 22, npm and git. On macOS run xcode-select --install once; on Windows install the \"Desktop development with C++\" workload of Visual Studio Build Tools if rebuild:native asks for it. sh Copy git clone https://github.com/Hamzasohail123/sessionforge cd sessionforge npm install npm run rebuild:native npm run electron:dev"},{"t":"Quick start","g":"Getting started","u":"docs/getting-started/","h":[["The first-run tour","the-first-run-tour"],["Next steps","next-steps"]],"x":"Five steps from a fresh install to two logged-in roles side by side. Create a session. Click the + (New session) button, name it (for example \"Admin\"), pick a colour and environment. Log in. Go to your app and log in as that role. Open split view. Pick a layout with the layout buttons at the right of the session bar. Create another session. Repeat for \"Customer\" or other roles. Send a login from Chrome. In Chrome, click the SessionForge Import extension → Send to SessionForge . The login opens in a new session, in a pane. The New Session dialog: name, colour and environment. The first-run tour # On a new install, SessionForge shows a short tour of split view, Settings and the MCP server once. Skip tour or Escape ends it. Replay it from Settings → General → Show tour again . Next steps # Sessions : rename, close, reopen and delete. Connect your coding agent ."},{"t":"Sessions","g":"Using SessionForge","u":"docs/sessions/","h":[["Create","create"],["Rename and recolour","rename-and-recolour"],["Close and reopen","close-and-reopen"],["Delete permanently","delete-permanently"],["Where data lives","where-data-lives"]],"x":"A session is one logged-in identity: its own cookies, localStorage, IndexedDB and cache, in one Chromium partition. Create # Click New session , enter a name, pick a colour and an environment: Local, Staging, Production or Custom. A session's environment can't be changed after it is created. Rename and recolour # Double-click a session in the session bar (or focus it and press F2 ), type, then Enter saves and Escape cancels. Clicking elsewhere also saves; an empty name keeps the old one. While renaming, click the dot to pick a colour. Close and reopen # The × next to a session closes it after a confirmation. Its tabs close, but its logins stay on disk, and Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+T reopens it with its tabs. The same shortcut reopens closed tabs, newest first. SessionForge keeps your last 10 closed sessions and your last 25 closed tabs. Closing an 11th session deletes the oldest closed session for good, including its logins. Closing tabs never deletes a closed session. Delete permanently # Delete permanently in the close confirmation removes the session's cookies and storage. It can't be reopened. Where data lives # All session data stays on your computer: ~/.config/sessionforge/ on Linux, ~/Library/Application Support/sessionforge/ on macOS and %APPDATA%\\sessionforge\\ on Windows."},{"t":"Split view","g":"Using SessionForge","u":"docs/split-view/","h":[["Panes","panes"],["New tabs and popups","new-tabs-and-popups"]],"x":"Switch between single, two columns, two rows and a 2x2 grid with the layout buttons at the right of the session bar. Panes # Each pane shows one tab from any session, under a header with the session and page name. Click a pane (or press Cmd/Ctrl+1 to 4 ) to focus it. The tab bar, address bar and session bar then act on that pane. Clicking a session fills the focused pane with that session's last tab. Layout and pane contents are restored on the next start. New tabs and popups # Context-menu \"open in new tab\" items, Cmd/Ctrl+U and window.open() popups open in the pane they came from, in the same session. DevTools open in a separate window. One Google account per Chrome profile. When several Google accounts are signed in to one Chrome profile, every session sent to SessionForge from that profile carries all of them, and signing out in one can sign out the others."},{"t":"Environment safety","g":"Using SessionForge","u":"docs/environment-safety/","h":[["What production changes","what-production-changes"]],"x":"Mark real production logins as Production. They are labelled red, and agents can only read them unless you confirm. A production session in the session bar. What production changes # A red PROD label in the session bar and in the pane header. Agents get read-only access unless you confirm by typing the session name in Settings → MCP Server / Agent Bridge. The confirmation lasts until SessionForge restarts. The environment can't be changed after a session is created, so a production session can't lose this protection. Renaming keeps it, and a closed production session comes back read-only for agents."},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","g":"Using SessionForge","u":"docs/keyboard-shortcuts/","h":[],"x":"Shortcuts work both in the SessionForge toolbar and inside pages, and act on the focused pane. Cmd on macOS, Ctrl elsewhere; Ctrl+Tab is Ctrl on every OS. Shortcut Action Cmd/Ctrl+K Command palette Cmd/Ctrl+, Open Settings Cmd/Ctrl+T New tab Cmd/Ctrl+W Close tab Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+T Reopen closed tab or session Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab Next / previous tab Cmd/Ctrl+1 – 4 Focus pane 1–4 Cmd/Ctrl+L Focus address bar Cmd/Ctrl+R , F5 Reload Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+R , Shift+F5 Hard reload (ignore cache) Alt+Left / Alt+Right Back / forward Escape Stop loading (while a page loads; otherwise the page gets it) F12 , Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+I Toggle DevTools Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+J DevTools console Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+C Inspect element Cmd/Ctrl+U View page source A combination with extra modifiers (for example Ctrl+Shift+K ) goes to the page. The SessionForge window's own DevTools are in the menu (press Alt , then View → Toggle Developer Tools)."},{"t":"Signing in with Google","g":"Using SessionForge","u":"docs/google-sign-in/","h":[],"x":"Google blocks Google account sign-in inside embedded browsers on purpose, and SessionForge is one. You see \"Couldn't sign you in. This browser or app may not be secure.\" SessionForge does not disguise itself to get around this: its user agent and fingerprint stay as they are. Instead, when a pane reaches a Google sign-in page, a banner in that pane offers Sign in with Chrome . The banner on a pane that reached a sign-in page. The full flow, requirements and limits are on Sign in with Chrome . Unverified: Chrome can bind Google sessions to the device (Device Bound Session Credentials). If your Google session is bound this way, the copy in SessionForge may sign out after a while; sign in with Chrome again if it does."},{"t":"Chrome extension","g":"Chrome extension","u":"docs/chrome-extension/","h":[["How it works","how-it-works"],["Install","install"]],"x":"SessionForge Import copies the login of the current Chrome tab into SessionForge with one click. SessionForge creates a new session from it and opens the page in a pane, already logged in. The popup, ready to send. How it works # Log in to a site in Chrome as usual. Click the SessionForge Import icon. The first time, a short notice explains what it reads, when, where it goes and how to stop it. Nothing is read or sent until you click Agree . Click Send to SessionForge . The first time you send from a site, Chrome asks you to allow access to that site. SessionForge names the session after the e-mail address in the tab title, or else the hostname, and opens the URL in a free pane. Not copied: sessionStorage, IndexedDB, history, bookmarks, saved passwords. Cookies without an expiry date are kept for 30 days, so imported logins survive a restart. Install # Install it from the Chrome Web Store . To work on the extension, load it unpacked: Get the chrome-extension/ folder (clone the repo, or download the source zip from the release). Open chrome://extensions/ and turn on Developer mode . Click Load unpacked and select the folder. Built and tested for Chrome. Edge and Brave should work but are untested. Firefox is not supported. Every popup state, with pictures: the extension page ."},{"t":"Sign in with Chrome","g":"Chrome extension","u":"docs/sign-in-with-chrome/","h":[["Requirements and limits","requirements-and-limits"]],"x":"Sign in where Google allows it, in Chrome, and send the login into the SessionForge session that asked for it. In the pane's banner, click Sign in with Chrome . Your browser opens the right Google sign-in page (or the site's login page for \"Sign in with Google\" on another site). Sign in there as usual. Click the SessionForge Import extension and choose Send to <session> (SessionForge asked for this) . The button for the waiting session shows the domains it sends. The login goes into the session that asked for it (no new session), and the waiting pane continues where it left off. From the keyboard: press Cmd/Ctrl+L , then Tab until the banner's button is focused, and Enter . Requirements and limits # SessionForge Import 1.2.0 or later in the browser that opens. SessionForge tries Chrome, then Chromium, Edge and Brave, else your default browser. SessionForge's import server on its default port 34567 (not changed with SESSIONFORGE_IMPORT_PORT ). A request is valid for 10 minutes and works once. Only cookies are sent for a request. Sites that keep their login in localStorage or IndexedDB stay signed out; use Send to new session for those. For your own app the site must use https, except on localhost."},{"t":"Extension privacy and permissions","g":"Chrome extension","u":"docs/extension-privacy/","h":[["What it reads, and when","what-it-reads-and-when"],["Where it goes","where-it-goes"],["Permissions","permissions"]],"x":"No site access at install, nothing read before you click Send, and nothing sent anywhere except SessionForge on 127.0.0.1. What it reads, and when # Send to SessionForge: the cookies of the site you send (what Chrome sends to that page, plus the site's domain cookies), that page's localStorage, and the page URL, title and favicon URL. Send to <session>: only the cookies of the domains shown on that button. Only when you click a Send button. Nothing is read in the background. Where it goes # Only to SessionForge on your computer: POST http://127.0.0.1:34567/import . Opening the popup also asks GET http://127.0.0.1:34567/pending which sessions wait for a login. After you agreed, at most once a day, it asks GitHub for the latest SessionForge version number; that request carries none of your data. No analytics, telemetry or remote code. Permissions # Permission Why activeTab The current tab's address and title in the popup cookies Read the site's cookies when you click Send scripting Read the page's localStorage when you click Send storage Your answer to the notice and the last version check http://127.0.0.1:34567/* Talk to SessionForge's import server *://*/* (optional) Asked per site, only when you send from it To stop it, remove site access in chrome://extensions → SessionForge Import → Details → Site access, or uninstall it. Full statement: store/PRIVACY.md ."},{"t":"Extension troubleshooting","g":"Chrome extension","u":"docs/extension-troubleshooting/","h":[],"x":"The common popup messages and how to fix them. Message What to do SessionForge is not running (could not reach 127.0.0.1:34567) Start SessionForge; check that nothing else uses port 34567 Chrome did not allow access to …, so nothing was sent Click Send again and allow access, or click Allow access in a tab Only http(s) pages can be sent The tab is a chrome:// page, the Web Store or a file; open the site itself Session too large to import (over 5 MB) Import from a page with less localStorage, or log in inside SessionForge This request expired or was already used Click Sign in with Chrome in SessionForge again No \"Send to <session>\" button Update or reload the extension (1.2.0 or later); check that SessionForge uses port 34567 Imported but not logged in The site keeps its login elsewhere (IndexedDB, sessionStorage). Log in once inside SessionForge All messages: chrome-extension/README.md ."},{"t":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","g":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","u":"docs/mcp/","h":[["Defaults","defaults"],["Limitations","limitations"]],"x":"An MCP server inside SessionForge at http://127.0.0.1:34568/mcp that lets your coding agent use the sessions you are logged in to. An agent can list your sessions, open pages in panes, read a compact page outline with element refs, click and type with real input events, read console and network logs, take screenshots, change the split layout, and send one HTTP request as several roles to compare what each gets. SessionForge has no AI of its own. The Agent label and Stop button in the pane an agent is using. Defaults # Off until you turn on Enable MCP server in Settings → MCP Server / Agent Bridge. New sessions start at Read access. Production sessions are read-only. Set SESSIONFORGE_MCP_PORT to use another port. Limitations # The MCP server first shipped in v0.1.0. It has no arbitrary JavaScript, file uploads, downloads or cross-origin iframe support. Next: set it up in your editor ."},{"t":"Set up Cursor, Codex or Claude Code","g":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","u":"docs/mcp-setup/","h":[["1. Turn on the server","1-turn-on-the-server"],["2. Export the token","2-export-the-token"],["3. Add the server to your editor","3-add-the-server-to-your-editor"],["Cursor","cursor"],["Codex","codex"],["Claude Code","claude-code"],["4. Restart and check","4-restart-and-check"]],"x":"Three steps: turn on the server, export the token, add the server to your editor. 1. Turn on the server # Open Settings (gear icon or Cmd/Ctrl+, ) → MCP Server / Agent Bridge and turn on Enable MCP server . 2. Export the token # Click Copy export line and add it to ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc : sh Copy export SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN='sfa_…' 3. Add the server to your editor # The panel shows the same snippets with your real port and path. Cursor # .cursor/mcp.json in a project, or ~/.cursor/mcp.json for all projects: json Copy { \"mcpServers\": { \"sessionforge\": { \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:34568/mcp\", \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer ${env:SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN}\" } } } } Codex # The CLI, VS Code extension and app share ~/.codex/config.toml : toml Copy [mcp_servers.sessionforge] url = \"http://127.0.0.1:34568/mcp\" bearer_token_env_var = \"SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN\" Claude Code # Run once. Keep the single quotes: they store ${SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN} literally, so the token never lands in ~/.claude.json . sh Copy claude mcp add --transport http --scope user sessionforge http://127.0.0.1:34568/mcp --header 'Authorization: Bearer ${SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN}' 4. Restart and check # Restart the editor from a shell that has SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN (or log out and in). Then check that the tools are listed: Cursor: Settings → MCP. Codex: codex mcp list , or /mcp in the chat. Claude Code: claude mcp list should say \"✔ Connected\", or /mcp in a session. Optional: give your agent the rules in docs/agents/sessionforge.md ."},{"t":"stdio bridge","g":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","u":"docs/stdio-bridge/","h":[["Cursor","cursor"],["Codex","codex"],["Claude Code","claude-code"],["Without Node","without-node"]],"x":"bin/sessionforge-mcp.mjs is a dependency-free bridge (Node 18 or later) that forwards to the running app. Its path is shown in the Settings panel. Cursor # json Copy { \"mcpServers\": { \"sessionforge\": { \"type\": \"stdio\", \"command\": \"node\", \"args\": [ \"<path>/sessionforge-mcp.mjs\" ], \"env\": { \"SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN\": \"${env:SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN}\" } } } } Codex # toml Copy [mcp_servers.sessionforge] command = \"node\" args = [\"<path>/sessionforge-mcp.mjs\"] env_vars = [\"SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN\"] Claude Code # Remove an HTTP entry first with claude mcp remove sessionforge ; the name must come before --env : sh Copy claude mcp add sessionforge --transport stdio --scope user --env 'SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN=${SESSIONFORGE_TOKEN}' -- node '<path>/sessionforge-mcp.mjs' Without Node # Use the SessionForge executable as command and add ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 to the server's environment. Packaged builds keep the bridge in resources/bin/ ."},{"t":"Approvals in your editor","g":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","u":"docs/approvals/","h":[["Files","files"]],"x":"Each editor decides on its own whether to ask before it runs an MCP tool. Settings → MCP Server → Editors → Approvals offers three presets per editor. Ask every time. Auto-run read-only tools (recommended): list_sessions , snapshot , wait_for , screenshot , console_logs , network_log , get_layout . Auto-run all SessionForge tools , for sessions you deliberately set to Control. Each preset shows the exact text to paste. Apply edits the file for you after a preview: it makes a <file>.bak-YYYYMMDD-HHmmss backup, changes only SessionForge entries, keeps comments, and never writes the token. SessionForge's own limits (Hidden/Read/Control, read-only production, Stop) apply whatever you choose. Files # Cursor: ~/.cursor/permissions.json , mcpAllowlist . Once this key exists, Cursor uses it instead of the allowlist in Cursor Settings, so tools you allowed there for other servers ask again. Claude Code: ~/.claude/settings.json , permissions.allow entries mcp__sessionforge__<tool> . Codex: default_tools_approval_mode in [mcp_servers.sessionforge] of ~/.codex/config.toml . Restart Codex afterwards. Details for each editor: README, Approvals in your editor ."},{"t":"Tools reference","g":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","u":"docs/mcp-tools/","h":[["A typical flow","a-typical-flow"]],"x":"The MCP server has 17 tools. Read tools never change a page; Control tools act in the session. Tool Access What it does list_sessions Read Your sessions (already logged in) with their tabs and your access. Call it first. open Control Open a URL in a session and show it in a pane. navigate Control Navigate a session tab to a URL. back Control Go back in a session tab's history. reload Control Reload a session tab. snapshot Read A compact page outline with element refs for click, type, select and press. click Control Click an element (ref or CSS selector) with a real mouse event. type Control Type into a text field; optionally press Enter. select Control Choose options of a <select> by value or label. press Control Press one key, optionally with modifiers. wait_for Read Wait for text, a selector, a URL or network idle. screenshot Read PNG of a session tab, or of all panes. For visual checks. console_logs Read Console messages of a session tab since the bridge was enabled. network_log Read Requests of a session tab with status and timing; secret headers redacted. send_request_as Read for GET, HEAD, OPTIONS; Control for others Send one HTTP request as several sessions and compare the answers. get_layout Read The split layout: mode, active pane, what each pane shows. set_layout Control Change the layout mode, which session each pane shows, and the focused pane. A typical flow # list_sessions → see Admin and Customer and your access to each. open { session: \"Admin\", url: \"http://localhost:3000/billing\" } . snapshot { session: \"Admin\" } → find refs such as [ref=e12] . click { session: \"Admin\", ref: \"e12\" } . send_request_as { sessions: [\"Admin\", \"Customer\"], include_no_session: true, url: \"http://localhost:3000/api/billing\" } → one column per role, with a summary of what differs. Not available: arbitrary JavaScript, file uploads, downloads, CDP, cross-origin iframes. snapshot does not look inside closed shadow DOM."},{"t":"Security model","g":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","u":"docs/mcp-security/","h":[],"x":"Agents act with a session's real cookies, so what an agent does is done as that logged-in user. These limits apply whatever your editor allows. Off by default. Nothing listens until you turn it on. 127.0.0.1 only. Only 127.0.0.1 / localhost Host headers are accepted, and every request with an Origin header is refused, so web pages can't reach it. Bearer token. 256-bit, stored encrypted with the OS keyring. Rotate it in the panel; old clients get 401. Per-session access: Hidden, Read or Control. New sessions start at Read. Hidden sessions are not listed and can't be addressed. Production is read-only unless you type the session name to confirm; that lasts until SessionForge restarts. URL guards apply to agent navigation, requests, redirects and popups. Agents can never call SessionForge's own import or MCP server. Stop. A \"🤖 Agent\" label and a Stop button appear in the pane an agent uses; Stop sets the session back to Read and ends the running call. The activity log lists every call. Redaction. Cookie, Set-Cookie, Authorization and other secret headers, query parameters and JSON keys are redacted; page content is labelled untrusted; calls are rate limited. There is no eval, CDP, file or shell tool. Known limits. Read access allows GET, HEAD and OPTIONS through send_request_as , and a GET can still have side effects (for example /logout ). Local malware with your user's rights can read session data. See SECURITY.md ."},{"t":"MCP troubleshooting","g":"MCP Server / Agent Bridge","u":"docs/mcp-troubleshooting/","h":[["Port in use","port-in-use"],["401 after rotating the token","401-after-rotating-the-token"],["\"SessionForge is not running, or the Agent Bridge is turned off\"","sessionforge-is-not-running-or-the-agent-bridge-is-turned-off"],["ui_busy","ui-busy"]],"x":"The four problems people hit most often. Port in use # Another SessionForge (or app) has port 34568. Quit it, or set SESSIONFORGE_MCP_PORT , then toggle the bridge off and on. 401 after rotating the token # Copy the new export line, update your shell profile and restart the editor. \"SessionForge is not running, or the Agent Bridge is turned off\" # From the stdio bridge: start SessionForge and enable Settings → MCP Server / Agent Bridge. ui_busy # Settings, a dialog or a notification covers the panes. Input tools pause until it closes; read tools keep working."},{"t":"Roadmap","g":"Project","u":"docs/roadmap/","h":[["Next up, in order","next-up-in-order"],["Later (evaluating)","later-evaluating"]],"x":"This roadmap is a plan, not a promise. Feedback in issues and discussions changes the order. Next up, in order # Next M2 Network log and request rules: a network drawer per session, header/block/redirect rules, Copy as cURL. Planned M3 \"Send as…\" panel: send one request as several sessions and compare the answers. Planned M4 Device emulation per pane: device presets and throttling, also as an agent tool. Planned M5 Launch kit: signed and notarized builds once there is traction. Later (evaluating) # Ideas such as exporting a session as a Playwright storageState, a bug bundle of all panes, and workspaces with session templates. None of these is promised. Full list: ROADMAP.md ."},{"t":"Contributing","g":"Project","u":"docs/contributing/","h":[["Set up","set-up"],["Tests","tests"]],"x":"Contributions are welcome. Open an issue first for anything large. Set up # sh Copy git clone https://github.com/Hamzasohail123/sessionforge.git cd sessionforge npm install npm run rebuild:native npm run electron:dev Tests # sh Copy npm run test:e2e This builds the app and runs the Playwright suite: unit specs and Electron end-to-end specs, with a temporary profile and a local test server. Run the unit specs alone with npx playwright test --project=unit . Code style, commit conventions and the review process: CONTRIBUTING.md and DEVELOPMENT.md ."},{"t":"Privacy","g":"Project","u":"docs/privacy/","h":[["Stored on your computer","stored-on-your-computer"]],"x":"SessionForge is a local-first desktop application. No data about you leaves your machine. No analytics, usage statistics, crash reports, telemetry, accounts or cloud sync. One update check a day: an HTTPS GET to GitHub's latest-release API with the User-Agent SessionForge/<version> , no cookies or identifiers. Turn it off in Settings → General → Check for updates . When an agent calls send_request_as , SessionForge sends that request, to the URL the agent chose, with the cookies of the sessions it named. Stored on your computer # Session data (cookies, localStorage, IndexedDB, cache) and a local SQLite database with names, colours and tabs. The Agent Bridge token is stored encrypted with the OS keyring. The activity log and the console and network logs that agents read live in memory only. This website has no analytics or cookies either. Full policy: PRIVACY.md ."},{"t":"Security","g":"Project","u":"docs/security/","h":[["Report a vulnerability","report-a-vulnerability"],["Best practices","best-practices"],["Known limits","known-limits"]],"x":"Please do not report security vulnerabilities through public GitHub issues. Report a vulnerability # Use GitHub Security Advisories . You should receive a response within 48 hours. The project follows a 90-day disclosure timeline. Supported: 0.1.x. Best practices # Keep it updated from GitHub Releases . SessionForge tells you about new versions; it does not install them. Use OS-level disk encryption. Log out of production sessions when you are not testing. Known limits # Local malware with your user's rights can read session data. Agents act with the session's real cookies. Closed shadow DOM and cross-origin iframes are not inspected by snapshot . Full policy: SECURITY.md . The MCP server's limits: Security model ."}]
